From c52ad3fb28a7d29780de5525fa2fd84d48528998 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 03:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] nvkms: a mode set that the display engine does not finish,
 said, and its FRL link trained again

An RTX 4080 SUPER (driver 620.70.06, an
LG C5 over HDMI) stopped showing anything three times, each time at the
mode set from the TMDS mode of a video (23.976 or 25 Hz) back to the
3840x2160 at 120 Hz of the interface, over HDMI FRL at 4 lanes of 8 Gb/s:
31 such mode sets that day, 3 of them hung. In each the link trained (no
"HDMI FRL link training failed."), the kernel log said how the mode went
out, and 3 s later NVKMS wrote "Lost display notification (0:0x00000000);
continuing.": EvoCheckNotifier() found the core channel's methods all
fetched (GET == PUT) and its completion notifier never written, wrote it
itself and went on as if the update were done. The window channel of that
head stopped then ("Error while waiting for GPU progress: 0x0000c67e:4
2:0:948:936", window 4 of head 2, every 5 s), nvidia-drm's commits timed
out on their flips or were refused (-22), and the machine had to be turned
off. In 90 HDMI mode sets whose
captured kernel logs time them, the InfoFrames that follow the update went
out 17 to 246 ms after the link trained. NVIDIA's
own code says what driving an OR with PROTOCOL_HDMI_FRL over a link that is
not trained does (the comment on bFakeLt in nvHdmiFrlSetConfig(): it hangs
the display hardware), and how a link that a head drives is trained again
without that: inside FRL flush mode, with the training that RM may not skip
(performLinkTraningToAssessFRLLink() of nvhdmipkt_C671.c; for DisplayPort,
the comment in DPSerializerLinkTrain()). Why the display engine did not
finish those three updates is not known: the training "took", and the log
says nothing of what RM did or of what the display said.

So that the next one says, and has a chance to come back:

- The mode line of an HDMI FRL head (nvHadesLogHdmiLink(), 9044) says how
  its link trained: in how many ms, at which of NVIDIA's six tries, and
  whether RM skipped the training (bLtSkipped of SET_HDMI_FRL_CONFIG, which
  the HDMI library now keeps: NvHdmi_HadesFrlLtSkipped()).
- After the update of a mode set, one line for each HDMI head of it: "the
  display engine finished its mode set N ms after its update went out".
- When the display engine has not finished it 1 s after the update went
  out, with the core channel's methods all fetched, a line for each HDMI
  head of it: the link (TMDS, or FRL and how long before the update its link
  trained), the VRR of its timings, its raster (three looks 2 ms apart at
  the line it scans: runs, stands, or in blanking each time) and what its
  display says over SCDC (Update_0 with FRL_start and FLT_update, FRL_Rate,
  Status_Flags 0 to 2 with the clock, the lanes locked and FLT_ready; the
  raw bytes too). Where a head of it drives HDMI FRL, its link is then
  trained again inside FRL flush mode with bDoNotSkipLt, as NVIDIA's HDMI
  library does for a link that a head drives, and where that is refused,
  with the fallback that nvHdmiFrlSetConfig() already has (bFakeLt), at the
  same rate; a line says each step. NVIDIA's 3 s are not made longer.
- When NVIDIA's 3 s run out all the same, the same line again, before
  NVIDIA's "Lost display notification", and the line after the update says
  that the display engine had not finished it.

The retraining is on by default; nvidia-modeset.hades_frl_stall_retrain=0
(also writable at run time, by root) leaves the mode set to NVIDIA's wait
alone, with the lines, to compare. Nothing changes for a mode set that the
display engine finishes within 1 s, nor for one without an HDMI head.

The decisions (when to look and when to train again; what three looks at
the raster say; the SCDC fields) are plain functions in nvkms-hades.h:
nvHadesModesetStallAction(), nvHadesRasterOfLooks() and the
NV_HADES_SCDC_* macros. A host test built against the header of this tree
checks them (29 checks: the threshold against the 17 to 246 ms seen, waits
before it, with methods still to fetch and once looked at; retraining only
with an FRL head and the parameter on; the raster; the SCDC bits) and fails
on each of 12 mutations of them; the header before this change does not
build it. The five modules build against Linux 7.3.5 with the compiler of
the image; the only warnings that are new are objtool's "call without
frame pointer save/setup" of the new functions, which every function of
NVIDIA's NVKMS object has. Not run on a GPU: whether training the link
again lets the display engine finish the update can only be seen on the
card that hangs.
